In article <deKdnUC--LtXFbPcRVn-qw@comcast.com>, "stewart allen" <stewaetallen@comcast.net> writes:
:will a vt320 work with a VAX 8600 mainframe

  A VT320 terminal might be used as a doorstop for the cabinet doors of
  a VAX 8600 (or it might be too low to the floor), as a serial terminal,
  and for various other tasks involving the operating systems usually
  found running on a VAX 8600 system, but the plastic shell of the VT320
  terminal is likely not sufficiently rated to act as a leveling wedge 
  for a computer system as heavy as the VAX 8600 series.  :-) 

  Did you have a specific task in mind?  :-)

  The serial console for the VAX 8600 series was usually a printer such
  as the LA100 series, but a serial terminal such as a VT320 should work.
  There can also be assorted (usually UNIBUS or DECserver LAT) serial 
  controllers configured with a VAX 8600, and -- if the operating system
  supports the serial controller or the terminal server in use -- the
  VT320 should work fine.

  For the serial wiring pinouts, please see the OpenVMS Frequently Asked
  Questions (FAQ) document and related materials.

  One-line questions are often surprisingly difficult to answer, FWIW.
  Details, background, operating system information, etc., can all help
  tailor the answer to the question.

